  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Growing Sinusoids",
    "lesson_core_move": "Classify growing sinusoids within $e^{st}$ by recognizing $\\sigma>0$ in $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$, interpreting $e^{\\sigma t}$ as the increasing envelope and $\\omega$ as the oscillation rate.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Growing Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Growing Sinusoids",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Define $x(t)=e^{st}$ as a continuous-time signal with $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$ held fixed and $t$ as the variable. In this lesson, we isolate the case $\\sigma>0$ so the same formula can be read as a growing sinusoid.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{(1+j\\omega)t}=e^t e^{j\\omega t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the simple base case $s=1+j\\omega$, the signal already splits into a real exponential factor and an oscillatory complex exponential factor. That is the pattern to keep in view before generalizing to arbitrary $\\sigma>0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{st}=e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}=e^{\\sigma t}e^{j\\omega t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When $\\sigma>0$, the factor $e^{\\sigma t}$ increases with $t$, so it acts as the envelope of the signal. The factor $e^{j\\omega t}$ supplies the oscillation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|e^{st}|=e^{\\sigma t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That magnitude formula ties the envelope directly to the real part of $s$: $\\sigma$ controls the growth, and $\\omega$ controls the oscillation rate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the classification rule is simple: if $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$ and $\\sigma>0$, then $e^{st}$ is a growing sinusoid with increasing envelope $e^{\\sigma t}$ and oscillation rate $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Growing Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Positive $\\sigma$ in $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Classify $x(t)=e^{(2+j3)t}$ by identifying $\\sigma$ and $\\omega$ and deciding whether the signal grows, decays, or stays constant.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by reading the exponent in standard form. In $x(t)=e^{(2+j3)t}$, the fixed parameter is $s=2+j3$, so the cue is a positive real part.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=2+j3=\\sigma+j\\omega,\\qquad \\sigma=2,\\ \\omega=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $\\sigma=2>0$, the real part is positive. That means the exponential factor is growing, not decaying or staying constant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{(2+j3)t}=e^{2t}e^{j3t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The factor $e^{2t}$ is the increasing envelope, and $e^{j3t}$ is the oscillatory factor. The number $3$ sets the oscillation rate, while $2$ sets the growth rate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|x(t)|=e^{2t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal is a growing sinusoid: positive $\\sigma$ gives growth, and $\\omega$ still controls the oscillation rate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Growing Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Separating $e^{\\sigma t}$ from $e^{j\\omega t}$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $e^{(1.5+j4)t}$ as a product and name which factor controls growth and which factor controls oscillation.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by reading the exponent in standard form. Here $s=1.5+j4$, so the cue is to split the real part from the imaginary part.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=1.5+j4=\\sigma+j\\omega,\\qquad \\sigma=1.5,\\ \\omega=4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply the split inside $e^{st}$. The real part becomes the growth factor $e^{\\sigma t}$, and the imaginary part stays in the oscillatory factor $e^{j\\omega t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{(1.5+j4)t}=e^{1.5t}e^{j4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The factor $e^{1.5t}$ controls the increasing envelope because its magnitude grows with $t$. The factor $e^{j4t}$ controls the oscillation rate.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the product form makes the roles explicit: $\\sigma=1.5$ gives growth, and $\\omega=4$ gives oscillation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Growing Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting $e^{\\sigma t}$ as an Increasing Envelope",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{0.5t}e^{j4t}$, identify the envelope and explain how it changes as $t$ increases.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $x(t)=e^{0.5t}e^{j4t}$, match the real exponential factor $e^{\\sigma t}$ to the envelope. Here that factor is $e^{0.5t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{0.5t}e^{j4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $\\sigma=0.5>0$, so the envelope is increasing rather than shrinking or staying constant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|x(t)|=e^{0.5t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "As $t$ increases, $e^{0.5t}$ gets larger, so the oscillation keeps the same rate but rides inside a widening envelope.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the envelope is $e^{0.5t}$, and the positive real part is exactly what makes it grow.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Growing Sinusoids",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Reading Growth Rate and Oscillation Rate Together",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{0.75t}e^{j8t}$, state the growth rate, the oscillation rate, and the overall classification.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by reading the exponent in standard form. The cue is that $s=0.75+j8$, so the real part gives growth and the imaginary part gives oscillation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=0.75+j8=\\sigma+j\\omega,\\qquad \\sigma=0.75,\\ \\omega=8",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $\\sigma=0.75>0$, the envelope is $e^{0.75t}$ and it increases with time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|x(t)|=e^{0.75t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the growth rate is $0.75$, the oscillation rate is $8$, and the signal is a growing sinusoid with envelope $e^{0.75t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
